## Pre-Flight Plan Review

Before dispatching Task 1, scan the plan once for conflicts:

- tasks that contradict each other or the plan's Global Constraints
- anything the plan explicitly mandates that the review rubric treats as a
  defect (a test that asserts nothing, verbatim duplication of a logic block)
- **Working-tree cleanliness:** before Task 1, run `git status --porcelain`. If it is non-empty, STOP and present the dirty entries to the user as a batched question — do NOT auto-stash, do NOT silently proceed. The user must choose one: (a) commit the pending changes themselves, (b) `git stash -u`, (c) explicitly accept "the entire current dirty tree is part of this run's baseline/scope" (recorded in the ledger), or (d) cancel. `git status` cannot attribute changes to "this ocmar run" vs pre-existing — any dirty state at start is pre-existing by definition. base must be a clean commit.

Present everything you find to your human partner as one batched question —
each finding beside the plan text that mandates it, asking which governs —
before execution begins, not one interrupt per discovery mid-plan. If the
scan is clean, proceed without comment. The review loop remains the net for
conflicts that only emerge from implementation.

## Stage-Skip Gates (lightweight path)

Not every plan needs the full gate chain. Before the Parallel-Admission Gate, classify the plan once:

**Lightweight path** — applies only when ALL hold: a single task, OR (≤ 2 files touched AND no public-interface / data-model / schema / permission / production-config change AND no cross-task dependency). On this path:
- **Skip the Parallel-Admission oracle dispatch** — a single task or sub-2-file plan cannot usefully parallelize; record the decision directly via `ocmar-state add-gate '{"gate_id":"parallel-admission","verdict":"skip","final_verdict":"SERIAL","phase_b":"skipped","reasoning":"lightweight: <why>"}'` instead of spending an oracle dispatch on an obvious SERIAL.
- **The task review IS the final review** — for a single-task lightweight plan, the task reviewer's SPEC+QUALITY verdict covers the whole branch (there is nothing cross-task to integrate); do NOT dispatch a separate final `_priv-oracle-grok`.

**Never skipped (every path, every tier):**
- The **fresh verifier** — even a one-line change gets a live verification run before "done".
- **High-risk changes are never lightweight**, regardless of file count: anything touching data/schema/permissions/production config, user-facing, or genuinely ambiguous takes the full path (independent final review + confirmation where relevant).
- The **fix loop** — Critical/Important findings still block their task.

**When in doubt → full path.** Mis-classifying a risky change as lightweight is the failure mode; over-classifying only wastes a little time.

## Parallel-Admission Gate

After the plan is written and before dispatching Task 1, decide whether this plan is worth running in **parallel lanes (C4)** or **serially** (the default flow). This gate answers "is parallel worth it" per-plan, automatically — instead of always paying C4's overhead or always running serial.

**Dispatch a fresh `oracle`** with this skill's [admission-gate.md](admission-gate.md) template, handing it `[PLAN_FILE]` (ownership matrix + task file sets + DAG dependencies) and `[BUILD_ISOLATION_PROFILE]` (project build system → isolation feasibility). It returns a terse block:

    VERDICT=PARALLEL | SERIAL | CONDITIONAL
    LANES=<suggested lane count, 0=SERIAL>
    LANE_SPLIT=<e.g. A:task-1,task-3 B:task-2,task-4; empty if SERIAL>
    CONFIDENCE=<high|medium|low>
    RISKS=<top risks>
    REASONING=<one line>

**Two layers — do not blur them:**
- **Phase A "is it worth it" = the oracle's LLM judgment** (8 factors: file overlap, DAG exec-dependencies, module boundaries, plan size, build-isolation feasibility, integration-conflict risk, resource cost, semantic coupling). The oracle only *advises* VERDICT/LANES/LANE_SPLIT.
- **Phase B "is it safe" = YOUR deterministic hard gate** (never delegate to the LLM): run the file-overlap check yourself — pairwise intersection of each candidate lane's file set (including lockfiles / generated files / build-config files) must be **empty**; verify LANE_SPLIT covers every task exactly once; verify diff authority. ∩≠∅ or coverage failure → force SERIAL or re-split.

**VERDICT=PARALLEL takes effect ONLY when Phase A returns PARALLEL AND Phase B passes.** The oracle's PARALLEL cannot bypass the file-overlap hard gate.

**Fail-closed:** dispatch failure / `CONFIDENCE=low` / Phase-B failure → **SERIAL** (slow serial beats a broken parallel integration). Record via `ocmar-state add-gate '{"gate_id":"parallel-admission","verdict":"pass","final_verdict":"<PARALLEL|SERIAL>","reasoning":"..."}'` (`verdict=pass` means the gate ran; `final_verdict` records the decision; full schema in admission-gate.md); a user waiver can override (recorded with `verdict:"waiver"`, never marks Phase B pass).

- **SERIAL** → continue the serial flow below.
- **PARALLEL** → jump to **Parallel Lane Execution (C4)** (after Final Verification Gate); the serial per-task loop is replaced by the lane flow.
- **CONDITIONAL** → re-split until Phase B passes for the parallel-eligible group; the rest run serially.

## Role Routing (no `model` parameter)

ocmar runs on opencode's `task` tool. Routing is **by role** (no single catch-all subagent type, no `model` parameter on `task`) — the agent definition fixes the model. Route each dispatch by role:

- **Implementer subagent** → `subagent_type="fixer"` (writes code, runs tests, records diff)
- **Spec-compliance reviewer / code-quality reviewer / final whole-branch reviewer** → `subagent_type="_priv-oracle-grok"` (judgment, architecture; writable oracle variant — writes its full review to `[REVIEW_FILE]`)
- **Exploration a subagent needs mid-task** → `subagent_type="explorer"` (read-only search)
- **Doc/API lookup a subagent needs** → `subagent_type="librarian"`
- **Final whole-branch verifier** → `subagent_type="_priv-verifier"` (runs project check command, read-only on source, produces build artifacts + log)

**Read-only exploration defaults to parallel (SHOULD):** when ≥2 mutually-independent read-only lookups are needed (e.g. two `explorer` searches in different areas, or `explorer` + `librarian`), dispatch them in the **same response as background tasks** and wait for all to complete before synthesizing — do NOT serialize independent reads. Serialize only when lookups have a dependency, when one result is enough, when quota is tight, or when the runtime does not support background subagents. The "no parallel implementation subagents" rule below is about *write* agents sharing one worktree; read-only `explorer`/`librarian` should parallelize.

**Capability preflight:** before dispatching in the background, confirm the runtime supports it (`OPENCODE_EXPERIMENTAL_BACKGROUND_SUBAGENTS=true`; the `task` tool errors if absent). If unsupported, fall back to serial silently — never pretend to parallelize.

If an implementer is genuinely out of its depth (architectural decision with multiple valid approaches, can't find clarity after reading the relevant files), escalate by re-dispatching an `oracle` to decide the approach first, then a fresh `fixer` to implement the decision — not by "selecting a bigger model".

### Per-task review dispatch model

Every task review is **one** fresh `_priv-oracle-grok` dispatch using `task-reviewer-prompt.md`, returning **SPEC** and **QUALITY** as two independent verdicts in one pass — for **all** task sizes, including ≤2-file tasks. "Two verdicts" means two output dimensions from one reviewer reading the diff once, **not** two reviewer dispatches. Never split one task into two reviewer dispatches: two dispatches double the review cost for no signal, and no separate spec-only or quality-only template exists. Large-diff dilution is solved by **task right-sizing** (writing-plans) — split the *task*, not the *review* — never by dispatching a second reviewer. (Two dispatches on a 1-file change double review cost for no signal — the original warning this section carried still holds, now as the universal rule.)

(Continuous execution is already covered above — do not pause between tasks.)

## Capability Profiles (model-tier → process depth)

ocmar is pinned to one model per role (the agent definition fixes it; routing is by role, never by selecting a model per task). Different model TIERS want different scaffolding depth: strong reasoning models chafe under prescribed intermediate steps; chat models benefit from more structure. Tag each role with a profile and let the profile modulate process depth — together with the task's risk tier (brainstorming) and the Stage-Skip Gates.

**Profiles:**
| Profile | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `reasoning_strong` | A reasoning/frontier model (Fable-class, GPT-5.x reasoning, Opus-tier). Strong internal planning — give it a clear goal + constraints + acceptance, prescribe FEWER intermediate steps. |
| `chat_strong` | A strong chat/workhorse model (the default coding tier). Benefits from the standard structure (plan, TDD, review checkpoints). |
| `cheap_fast` | A fast/cheap model (read-only retrieval, simple execution). High-volume low-judgment work; keep its scope narrow. |
| `high_assurance` | A TASK flag (not a model): forces the full path regardless of model tier — independent review, fresh verifier, explicit confirmation. |

**Role → profile (default mapping; update on model upgrade):**
| Role | Default profile |
|---|---|
| implementer (`fixer`) | `chat_strong` (or `reasoning_strong` if a frontier coding model backs it) |
| reviewer / oracle (`_priv-oracle-*`) | `reasoning_strong` (judgment task) |
| verifier (`_priv-verifier`) | `cheap_fast` + `high_assurance` (mechanical live-rerun, but never skipped) |
| explorer / librarian | `cheap_fast` |

**How profile modulates depth** (consulted by the controller at dispatch — a convention, not a runtime switch):
- A `reasoning_strong` implementer on a low-risk task → favor the **lightweight path** (Stage-Skip Gates) and do NOT prescribe every intermediate step; give the goal and let it plan.
- A `chat_strong` implementer → keep the standard structure (plan + TDD + review checkpoints).
- A `high_assurance` task (data/schema/permissions/prod/user-facing) → **full path regardless of model tier**: independent final review + fresh verifier + confirmation. Profile never downgrades a high-risk task.

**On model upgrade:** when a role's model changes, update its profile in this table, then run the [Harness-Decay Audit](harness-audit.md) — the profile shift is exactly the trigger that may turn load-bearing stages into dead weight.

## Pre-Final Spec Self-Check

Before the final whole-branch review, confirm every spec requirement has been delivered — do NOT let the final _priv-oracle-grok be the first place a missing requirement surfaces. The self-check is a hint map for the final reviewer, never a pass list.

**Dispatch a fresh read-only agent** (`explorer` or read-only `oracle` — not you) to build a self-check table from the review-package, the plan, and each task's report (all under `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/`; do not depend on commits — ocmar default does not commit):

| Criterion ID | Owner task | Artifact / report | file:line or test/command | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| T1-C1 | Task 1 | review-task-1.md | src/x.kt:42 | high |
| T3-C2 | Task 3 | review-task-3.md | (not found) | missing |

- **Status:** `high` (verified in a report/diff with file:line), `low` (claimed but no evidence), or `missing` (no artifact addresses it). Pull the IDs from the plan's Criterion Ownership Matrix.
- The agent reads artifacts only — it does not re-derive the diff or re-run anything. You hold the paths; the agent holds no session history. **Final-only exception:** a criterion marked Final-only (lives in unchanged code or spans tasks) may have no artifact evidence yet — for those, the agent does ONE focused `Read` of the specific file:line the plan's ownership matrix names, or reports `missing-evidence` so the plan is amended to produce the artifact. It never broad-crawls source.
- **Persist + register the table:** write the completed self-check to `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/self-check.md` and run `scripts/sdd-manifest <slug> self-check .ocmar/workflows/<slug>/self-check.md`. This file is the `[SELF_CHECK_FILE]` the final reviewer reads — without it registered, the reviewer has no coordinate pointer to where each requirement was delivered.

**Remediation, not annotation:** any `missing` or `low` criterion triggers the fix loop — dispatch a `fixer` for the gap, re-review that task, regenerate the affected review-package, THEN run the final whole-branch _priv-oracle-grok. Do NOT hand the _priv-oracle-grok a table of gaps and ask it to "note them" — gaps are fixed before final review, same as any failed spec review.

**Neutral framing:** the self-check table is a hint map, never a pass list. The final _priv-oracle-grok still reads the full review-package independently and may raise findings the self-check marked `high`. A `high` confidence does not license the _priv-oracle-grok to skip a criterion; it only points the _priv-oracle-grok at where each requirement was delivered.

## Final Verification Gate

**Hard precondition (review-first / verify-last):** the verifier runs ONLY after the final whole-branch `_priv-oracle-grok` review has returned `VERDICT=APPROVED` with **zero Critical and zero Important** findings. Verifying before review is forbidden — the verifier proves tests/build pass, NOT that the spec is satisfied (a green suite cannot substitute for review). Running verify before review wastes verifications: review findings force fixes that demand a fresh re-verify, so every pre-review verify is thrown-away cost.

The single exception: once the verifier itself FAILS (a test or build failure), you re-enter the `fixer → _priv-oracle-grok re-review → verifier` loop on that test/build failure — that is repairing a failing verification, not the regular review-then-verify order.

After the final whole-branch `_priv-oracle-grok` review passes, **before** `ocmar-finishing-a-development-branch`, dispatch a **fresh verifier** — this is the `ocmar-verification-before-completion` contract. It is distinct from the reviewers (judgment) and the implementers (write code):

- **Dispatch:** `task(subagent_type="_priv-verifier", prompt=<verifier prompt>, background=false)`. Use `_priv-verifier` — a dedicated read-only-on-source agent (bash:allow to run the check command; edit/write/apply_patch:deny). It is distinct from `fixer` (implementer, fully write-capable) and `_priv-oracle-grok` (reviewer; judgment + writes `[REVIEW_FILE]`). The read-only `oracle` is reserved for non-write judgment (admission-gate / architectural escalation / self-check / lane-judge).
- **Verifier prompt must hand it paths (not summaries):** the plan file, the final review-package diff file, each implementer's report file, and the exact verification command (ocdroid: `./scripts/check.sh`; other projects: their agreed command).
- **Verifier is read-only on source:** it runs the verification command, captures full output to a file under `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/verify-<ts>.log`, and returns **only** the exit code, failure count, and the log path. It must NOT edit source files.
- **Live rerun is mandatory:** the verifier must force a fresh execution — no `UP-TO-DATE` / `FROM-CACHE` test results count as evidence. ocdroid: `./scripts/check.sh --fresh` (the script forwards `--rerun-tasks --no-build-cache` to gradle). Other projects: the no-cache equivalent (gradle `--rerun-tasks`, pytest `-p no:cacheprovider`, go `go test -count=1`, jest `--no-cache`). The verifier prints `OCMAR_VERIFY_START=<epoch>` as the first line of its log; the gate rejects a log whose test tasks are all cached.
- **Gate:** proceed to finishing only on exit 0 AND 0 failures with the log path recorded in the ledger. Any failure → re-enter the fix loop (dispatch `fixer` on the failing task, re-review, re-verify). Do not let the verifier itself fix things — keep the role boundary clean.

This closes the loop: implementer (fixer, writes) → reviewer (_priv-oracle-grok, judges) → verifier (runs check, read-only on source) → finish. No completion claim reaches `finishing` without a fresh verification log.

## Parallel Lane Execution (C4)

When the Parallel-Admission Gate returns VERDICT=PARALLEL, the serial per-task loop is replaced by parallel lanes. Each lane is an independent git worktree with its own ocmar-state instance (isolated flock/fingerprint/checkpoint/restore via `--root`). Everything in Role Routing / Constructing Reviewer Prompts / File Handoffs still applies *inside* each lane.

**Lifecycle:**
1. **Init:** `scripts/ocmar-lane init <slug> <base> <main_branch>` (creates `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/lanes.json`; auto-detects build profile).
2. **Create lanes by wave:** `scripts/ocmar-lane create <lane-id> <task-ids> [--deps <lane-ids>] [--wave <n>]` per `LANE_SPLIT`. **Execution dependencies are wave-gated** (gpt): Wave 1 lanes fork from `base`; Wave N+1 fork from the main tip *after* Wave N integrated (so they see upstream artifacts). `ocmar-lane` refuses to create a dependent lane whose deps aren't integrated — never let dependent lanes fork from B0 in parallel.
3. **Per-lane build isolation:** `scripts/ocmar-lane env <lane-id>` prints a `build-env.sh` — every subagent in that lane must `source` it before building, AND pass `$OCMAR_BUILD_ARGS` on the build command (e.g. `./gradlew $OCMAR_BUILD_ARGS build`): `GRADLE_USER_HOME`/`.m2` take effect via env, but the Kotlin `-Pkotlin.compiler.execution.strategy=in-process` and repo-local flags in `OCMAR_BUILD_ARGS` only apply when explicitly passed on the command line (opus). This defeats gradle#8375 / ksp#1040 races.
4. **Per-lane subagent-driven flow:** inside each lane, dispatch `fixer`→`_priv-oracle-grok`(task-reviewer)→fix loop exactly as serial, but scoped to that lane's worktree (cwd) and ocmar-state instance (`ocmar-state <lane-state-dir> <cmd> --root <lane-worktree> --owner <sid>`). Briefs/reports/review-packages live under `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/lanes/<lane-id>/`. Lanes run **concurrently** (independent worktrees + flocks).
5. **Lane verifier:** when a lane's tasks are all `reviewed`, run `scripts/ocmar-lane verify <lane-id> --verifier '<check-command>' --owner <sid>` — this runs the lane-scoped verifier in that worktree (per-lane build-env sourced via absolute state_dir path), and **on pass atomically `promote-batch`es the lane's tasks (reviewed→verified) and marks `lane-verified`**. `--verifier` is required (never promote without verification evidence). **Never `ocmar-lane mark <lane-id> lane-verified` directly** — `verify` with `--verifier` is the only path to `lane-verified` (direct mark is refused).
6. **Main ledger dormant:** while lanes run, the main `state.json` is dormant (lanes.json is source of truth) — do not advance main ledger per-task state during parallel execution.
7. **Integrate by wave (serial):** `scripts/ocmar-lane next-wave` → lanes whose deps are all integrated; for each `scripts/ocmar-lane integrate <lane-id>`: lane-verified check → one checkpoint commit on lane branch → rebase onto main (`rerere` enabled) → `merge --ff-only` (linear; `--no-ff` for a revert-able merge commit). Conflict → lane `blocked` (exit 12). Integration is **serial**, one lane at a time. `ocmar-lane` records each lane's commit range for rollback.
8. **Rollback matrix:** pre-integrate → `ocmar-lane remove`; mid-integrate (before final gate) → `ocmar-lane reset-main <pre-integrate tip>`; post-final → `ocmar-lane revert-lane <lane-id>` (`git revert <start>^..<end>` for FF; `revert -m 1 <merge>` for --no-ff) + re-run final gate. Never split a lane after the final gate.
9. **Integrated final gate (F0 applies):** once ALL lanes integrated, run the **Pre-Final Spec Self-Check** (cross-lane) → **final whole-branch _priv-oracle-grok** (review-package = main vs base B0) → on APPROVED a **fresh full verifier** in the main worktree with the **default** environment (real integrated state; run `./gradlew --stop` first to clear lingering lane daemons). On pass → `ocmar-lane promote-batch-collect` feeds the main ledger's `promote-batch`.

Parallelism is in the implement/review phase; **integration is serial**. If integration keeps conflicting, the admission gate was wrong — fall back to serial for the remaining lanes. Each lane's artifacts are tagged by lane in the workflow manifest so the final reviewer misses nothing.

## Trial Parallelism (C3)

C3 is **trial parallelism**: the *same* task executed in N parallel lanes with *different* implementation approaches (different algorithms / architectures / libraries), and only the **winner** is integrated; losers are discarded. Unlike C2/C4 (different tasks, disjoint file-sets, all integrated), trial lanes **intentionally overlap on the same file-set** and **only the winner integrates**.

| Dimension | C2/C4 (independent tasks) | C3 (trial) |
|---|---|---|
| Parallel object | Multiple **different tasks** | **Same task**, multiple **approaches** |
| Inter-lane file-set | **Disjoint** (file-overlap is a hard gate) | **Intentionally overlapping** (same task, different impls) |
| Integration | All lanes integrate (serial) | **Only winner** integrates; losers → `loser` (worktree discarded, branch preserved) |
| Admission | parallel-admission gate (plan-level) | **trial-admission gate** (task-level, higher bar) |

**High bar — not the default.** C3 is *not* the first choice. Prefer coverage expansion / serial TDD / single-approach C2 lanes. Use C3 only when a task genuinely has **≥2 valid architectural approaches** AND serial try-each-and-revert cost exceeds parallel resource cost AND the approaches are **objectively comparable** (benchmark / clear acceptance criteria). When in doubt → serial.

**Flow:**

1. **Trial-admission gate (task-level).** When a task has ≥2 valid architectural approaches, record a `trial-admission` gate via `ocmar-state add-gate '{"gate_id":"trial-admission-<task>","verdict":"pass","approaches":[...],"reasoning":"..."}'`. Fail-closed: if not clearly worth trying in parallel, don't record the gate → serial TDD. (See admission-gate.md §"C3 Trial Admission" for the 3 thresholds.)

2. **Create trial lanes (one per approach).** `scripts/ocmar-lane create <lane-id> <task-id> --trial-group <group-id> --declared-files <overlap-files>`. All lanes in the same `--trial-group` work on the same task and **share the file-set by design** — the admission gate's file-overlap hard gate is **exempted for same-group lanes** (C3 design intent: same task, different impls, only winner integrates). The exemption also covers task-coverage (same task across same-group lanes is not a duplicate). Cross-group / trial-vs-non-trial overlap is still hard-blocked.

3. **Per-lane subagent-driven + verify (standard).** Inside each trial lane, run the standard flow: `fixer` → `_priv-oracle-grok` (task-reviewer) → fix loop, scoped to that lane's worktree. When a lane's tasks are all `reviewed`, run `scripts/ocmar-lane verify <lane-id> --verifier '<check-command>'`. Each trial lane produces its own report/benchmark/review-package artifacts under `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/lanes/<lane-id>/`.

4. **`select-winner <group> --prepare` (collect artifacts + judge prompt).** Once **all** trial lanes in the group are `lane-verified`, run `scripts/ocmar-lane select-winner <group> --prepare`. This is a **deterministic** script (no LLM): it validates all lanes are `lane-verified` (else exit 12), collects each lane's report/benchmark/review-package artifacts from its state_dir, and prints a terse **judge prompt** (TRIAL_GROUP / LANES / ARTIFACTS / CRITERIA / JUDGE).

5. **Dispatch oracle to judge (you do this, not ocmar-lane).** Hand the printed judge prompt + each lane's artifacts to a fresh `oracle`. The oracle reads artifacts, compares on acceptance criteria + benchmark, and returns `WINNER=<lane> REASONING=<text> CONFIDENCE=<high|medium|low>`.

6. **`select-winner <group> --winner <lane> --reasoning <text> --confidence <h|m|l>` (apply decision).** Deterministic execution of the oracle's verdict:
   - **Fail-closed:** `--confidence low` → exit 12 (route to human judgment; do not auto-pick).
   - Winner lane → `status=winner`; non-winner lanes → `status=loser`.
   - Loser discard: `git worktree remove --force <loser-wt>` (release build resources); **branch preserved** (`omos/<slug>-<lane>` not `-D`'d) for audit / cherry-pick / worktree rebuild.
   - Winner lane's state_dir records a **`trial-evaluation-<group>` gate** via `ocmar-state add-gate` (verdict=pass, winner/losers/reasoning/confidence) — audit trail in the standard gate system.
   - `lanes.json trial_groups[<group>] = {winner, losers, evaluated_ts, reasoning, confidence}`.

7. **Integrate the winner (standard integrate).** `scripts/ocmar-lane integrate <winner>` — winner flows through the normal integrate path (rebase onto main, ff-only merge). `integrate` refuses a `loser` lane (exit 12).

8. **Final gate (F0 applies).** After winner integration, run the standard **Pre-Final Spec Self-Check** → **final whole-branch _priv-oracle-grok** (review-package includes the trial-evaluation gate + each approach's lane report so the reviewer can audit the verdict's rationale) → on APPROVED a **fresh full verifier** in the main worktree.

**Safety properties:**
- **Loser branch preserved** — `git log omos/<loser-branch>` remains available; oracle mis-judgment is recoverable (cherry-pick or rebuild worktree). Only the worktree (build resources) is released.
- **Trial-evaluation gate audit** — the verdict (winner, losers, reasoning, confidence) is recorded in the winner's `ocmar-state` gates[] via the standard `add-gate` mechanism, traceable by final reviewer / resume / audit.
- **Trial exemption not abusable** — `--trial-group` is set explicitly at `create` time; only **same-group** lanes get the file-overlap / task-coverage exemption. Cross-group, trial-vs-non-trial, or unmarked lanes are still hard-gated.
- **Fail-closed on low confidence** — `select-winner apply --confidence low` exits 12; the controller routes to human judgment rather than auto-picking.

**`select-winner` is a deterministic ocmar-lane subcommand**, not a separate tool. Its verdict (gate), state transitions (winner/loser), and artifacts (manifest) all land in the existing ocmar system — final reviewer / resume / audit can trace them.

## Handling Implementer Status

Implementer subagents report one of four statuses. Handle each appropriately:

**DONE:** Generate the review package (`scripts/review-package BASE <slug> [HEAD|WORKTREE]`, from this skill's directory — it prints the unique file path it wrote). BASE is the commit SHA you recorded before dispatching the implementer — never `HEAD~1`, which silently drops all but the last commit of a multi-commit task. If the implementer committed, pass HEAD; if it did not commit (ocmar default — record diff, don't commit unless asked), omit HEAD and the script diffs BASE against the working tree (committed-since-base PLUS uncommitted). Then dispatch the task reviewer with the printed path.

**DONE_WITH_CONCERNS:** The implementer completed the work but flagged doubts. Read the concerns before proceeding. If the concerns are about correctness or scope, address them before review. If they're observations (e.g., "this file is getting large"), note them and proceed to review.

**NEEDS_CONTEXT:** The implementer needs information that wasn't provided. Provide the missing context and re-dispatch.

**BLOCKED:** The implementer cannot complete the task. Assess the blocker:
1. If it's a context problem, provide more context and re-dispatch the same role (`fixer`)
2. If the task requires more reasoning (architectural decision with multiple valid approaches), dispatch an `oracle` to decide the approach first, then re-dispatch a fresh `fixer` on the decision
3. If the task is too large, break it into smaller pieces
4. If the plan itself is wrong, escalate to the human

**Never** ignore an escalation or force a blind retry with no change. If the implementer said it's stuck, something needs to change (more context, a smaller scope, an upstream decision, or a different role).

## Handling Reviewer ⚠️ Items

The task reviewer may report "⚠️ Cannot verify from diff" items — requirements
that live in unchanged code or span tasks. These do not block the rest of the
review, but you must resolve each one yourself before marking the task
complete: you hold the plan and cross-task context the reviewer
lacks. If you confirm an item is a real gap, treat it as a failed spec
review — send it back to the implementer and re-review.

## Dispatch Failure & Reviewer Unavailable (fail-closed)

`task()` dispatch can itself fail (unknown agent type, model API timeout/quota, agent error before producing a status) — distinct from the four implementer business statuses. Handle it fail-closed:

- **Reviewer / verifier dispatch fails or the agent is unavailable:** STOP. Report "review/verify agent <name> unavailable: <reason>" and ask the user to decide: retry / switch to a pre-authorized reviewer fallback / grant a written waiver to defer. Do NOT proceed past the gate.
- **Implementer dispatch fails:** you may switch to `fixer-grok` / `fixer-gpter` ONLY after explicit user authorization (those agents' own definitions require it) — never auto-switch.
- **Never** substitute the controller's own self-assessment for a reviewer verdict. "User agreed to defer the review" is a waiver, not a pass — record it in the ledger via `ocmar-state add-gate` with `verdict:"waiver"` and a `user-approval-ref`; it never counts toward `verified`.
- **Never** rewrite a past reviewer's score. If a threshold isn't met, the options are: re-review after fixes / lower the formal target / user waiver — not editing history.
- Retry a failed dispatch at most once before escalating; blind infinite retries have no value.

(Mechanism-level enforcement of fail-closed is out of scope here and relies on prompt discipline + ledger audit; see project doc §7.9/§7.10 for the deterministic-gate path.)

## Constructing Reviewer Prompts

Per-task reviews are task-scoped gates. The broad review happens once, at the
final whole-branch review. When you fill a reviewer template:

- Do not add open-ended directives like "check all uses" or "run race tests
  if useful" without a concrete, task-specific reason
- Do not ask a reviewer to re-run tests the implementer already ran on the
  same code — the implementer's report carries the test evidence
- Do not pre-judge findings for the reviewer — never instruct a reviewer to
  ignore or not flag a specific issue. If you believe a finding would be a
  false positive, let the reviewer raise it and adjudicate it in the review
  loop. If the prompt you are writing contains "do not flag," "don't treat X
  as a defect," "at most Minor," or "the plan chose" — stop: you are
  pre-judging, usually to spare yourself a review loop.
- The global-constraints block you hand the reviewer is its attention
  lens. Copy the binding requirements verbatim from the plan's Global
  Constraints section or the spec: exact values, exact formats, and the
  stated relationships between components ("same layout as X", "matches
  Y"). The reviewer's template already carries the process rules (YAGNI,
  test hygiene, review method) — the constraints block is for what THIS
  project's spec demands.
- Hand the reviewer its diff as a file: run this skill's
  `scripts/review-package BASE <slug> [HEAD|WORKTREE]` and pass the reviewer the file path
  it prints (or, without bash: `git log --oneline`, `git diff --stat`,
  and `git diff -U10` for the range, redirected to one uniquely named
  file; if the implementer did not commit, `git diff -U10 BASE` against the
  working tree). The output never enters your own context, and the reviewer sees
  the commit list, stat summary, and full diff with context in one Read
  call. Use the BASE you recorded before dispatching the implementer —
  never `HEAD~1`, which silently truncates multi-commit tasks.
- A dispatch prompt describes one task, not the session's history. Do not
  paste accumulated prior-task summaries ("state after Tasks 1-3") into
  later dispatches — a real session's dispatch hit 42k chars of which 99%
  was pasted history. A fresh subagent needs its task, the interfaces it
  touches, and the global constraints. Nothing else.
- Dispatch fix subagents for Critical and Important findings. Record Minor
  findings as they arise — append each to `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/minor-ledger.md`
  and register it once: `scripts/sdd-manifest <slug> minor-ledger .ocmar/workflows/<slug>/minor-ledger.md`.
  The final whole-branch reviewer reads this file (its `[MINOR_LEDGER]` input) to triage which Minors must be fixed before merge. A roll-up nobody reads is a silent discard.
  **Severity → re-review rule:** only Critical and Important findings block a task and trigger a fix + re-review. Minor findings are recorded in the progress ledger as they arise, are NOT fixed immediately, and do NOT trigger a per-task re-review — the final whole-branch reviewer triages the accumulated Minor list and decides which must be fixed before merge (a roll-up nobody reads is a silent discard). Accumulated Minors of the same kind may be upgraded to Important at final triage.
- A finding labeled plan-mandated — or any finding that conflicts with
  what the plan's text requires — is the human's decision, like any plan
  contradiction: present the finding and the plan text, ask which governs.
  Do not dismiss the finding because the plan mandates it, and do not
  dispatch a fix that contradicts the plan without asking.
- The final whole-branch review gets a package too: run
  `scripts/review-package MERGE_BASE <slug> [HEAD|WORKTREE]` (MERGE_BASE = the commit the
  branch started from, e.g. `git merge-base main HEAD`) and include the
  printed path in the final review dispatch, so the final reviewer reads
  one file instead of re-deriving the branch diff with git commands.
- The final review dispatch MUST also hand the reviewer these paths (not
  summaries): `[MANIFEST_FILE]=.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/manifest.md`,
  `[SELF_CHECK_FILE]` (if the Pre-Final Spec Self-Check produced one), and
  `[MINOR_LEDGER]` (if any un-closed Minors exist). Without the manifest the
  reviewer sees only the branch diff and silently loses un-closed Minors and
  ⚠️ items — the exact failure the roll-up warning above guards against.
  (The template at `ocmar-requesting-code-review/code-reviewer.md` carries
  these placeholders; the controller fills them.)
- Every fix dispatch carries the implementer contract: the fix subagent
  re-runs the tests covering its change and reports the results. Name the
  covering test files in the dispatch — a one-line fix does not need the
  whole suite. Before re-dispatching the reviewer, confirm the fix report
  contains the covering tests, the command run, and the output; dispatch
  the re-review once all three are present.
- If the final whole-branch review returns findings, dispatch ONE fix
  subagent with the complete findings list — not one fixer per finding.
  Per-finding fixers each rebuild context and re-run suites; a real
  session's final-review fix wave cost more than all its tasks combined.

## File Handoffs

Everything you paste into a dispatch prompt — and everything a subagent
prints back — stays resident in your context for the rest of the session
and is re-read on every later turn. Hand artifacts over as files:

- **Task brief:** before dispatching an implementer, run this skill's
  `scripts/task-brief PLAN_FILE N <slug>` — it extracts the task's full text to a
  uniquely named file and prints the path. Compose the dispatch so the
  brief stays the single source of requirements. Your dispatch should
  contain: (1) one line on where this task fits in the project; (2) the
  brief path, introduced as "read this first — it is your requirements,
  with the exact values to use verbatim"; (3) interfaces and decisions
  from earlier tasks that the brief cannot know; (4) your resolution of
  any ambiguity you noticed in the brief; (5) the report-file path and
  report contract. Exact values (numbers, magic strings, signatures, test
  cases) appear only in the brief.
- **Report file:** name the implementer's report file after the brief
  (  brief `…/task-N-brief.md` → report `…/task-N-report.md`) and put it in
  the dispatch prompt. The implementer writes the full report there and
  returns only status, change summary (commits if it committed, or a
  one-line working-tree diff summary if it did not — ocmar default), a
  one-line test summary, and concerns.
- **Reviewer inputs:** the task reviewer gets three paths — the same brief
  file, the report file, and the review package — plus the global
  constraints that bind the task.
- **Reviewer return (terse):** the reviewer (_priv-oracle-grok) writes its full review to `[REVIEW_FILE]` (path given by the controller at dispatch, e.g. `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/review-task-N.md` or `review-final.md`) and returns ONLY a terse block (< 1KB) of pure `KEY=VALUE` lines (indented 4 spaces inside the prompt fence; **no nested code fence (three backticks) — it breaks the outer fence**):

      VERDICT=APPROVED | NEEDS_FIX
      SPEC=PASS | FAIL | UNVERIFIABLE
      QUALITY=APPROVED | NEEDS_FIX
      CRITICAL=<count>
      IMPORTANT=<count>
      MINOR=<count>
      REVIEW=[REVIEW_FILE]
      ONE_LINE=<one-sentence summary>

  Fail-closed: a missing/empty/malformed `[REVIEW_FILE]` (or terse block) is a dispatch failure (see Dispatch Failure section) — it blocks the gate and does NOT enter the fix loop. Controller rule: when `CRITICAL+IMPORTANT>0` or `VERDICT=NEEDS_FIX`, **Read `[REVIEW_FILE]` before dispatching the fixer** (counts alone are not enough). `SPEC=UNVERIFIABLE` → a ⚠️ item the controller resolves itself. The controller's dispatch prompt to the reviewer MUST include the `[REVIEW_FILE]` path and `[WORKFLOW_DIR]`.
- Fix dispatches append their fix report (with test results) to the same
  report file and return a short summary; re-reviews read the updated file.
- **Artifact manifest:** maintain a plain manifest at `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/manifest.md` (path + type per line: brief / report / review-package / review / self-check / minor-ledger / verify-log). `scripts/task-brief` and `scripts/review-package` auto-register their outputs. For every OTHER artifact you create (implementer report, per-task review, self-check, minor-ledger, verify-log), run `scripts/sdd-manifest <slug> <type> <path>` — do not hand-edit manifest.md. The final reviewer and verifier read the manifest so their inputs are complete (every un-closed Minor, every per-task report) rather than reconstructed from the controller's memory — this prevents silent drop of a review or a Minor that the controller forgot to forward.

## Durable Progress

Conversation memory does not survive compaction. In real sessions, controllers that lost their place have re-dispatched entire completed task sequences — the single most expensive failure observed. Track progress in a **per-slug ledger** with a consistent state machine, not only in todos.

**Slug (decide once, before Task 1):** derive a `slug` from the plan filename stem (e.g. `docs/ocmar/plans/2026-07-13-foo.md` → `foo`) or a user-given name. Pass this SAME slug to every `scripts/...` call AND every subagent prompt — implementer/reviewer/verifier prompts carry `[SLUG]` + `[WORKFLOW_DIR]` (see Prompt Templates). All artifacts live under `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/`.

**Task state machine:** each task transitions `implementing → reviewed → verified`.
- `implementing`: fixer working, or has not yet passed per-task review.
- `reviewed`: passed the per-task spec+quality review (_priv-oracle-grok). NOT yet final-verified.
- `verified`: the final whole-branch verifier (fresh `_priv-verifier` agent, runs project check) passed. Only `verified` is fully done.

The final whole-branch `_priv-oracle-grok` review runs once all tasks reach `reviewed`; only after it returns `VERDICT=APPROVED` with **zero Critical and zero Important** does the verifier run for that batch (review-first / verify-last — see Final Verification Gate). On pass it **atomically promotes that batch `reviewed` → `verified`** via a single `ocmar-state promote-batch <task-id>...` call (one flock + one load + one fingerprint recompute + one atomic replace — no partial-verified state on crash). On failure it demotes ONLY the affected task(s) to `implementing` (with the verifier's failing evidence). Those tasks are then fixed + re-reviewed → `reviewed` again, which triggers a fresh verifier run for the new batch. "Once" = once per pass through the reviewed set, not a single run for the whole session.

**Ledger (machine-authoritative):** all state lives in `.ocmar/workflows/<slug>/state.json`, managed exclusively by `scripts/ocmar-state` (see `/tmp/opencode/ocmar-state-contract.md` for the CLI). `progress.md` is rendered from it for humans — never hand-edit either file. Every state mutation goes through `ocmar-state`:

- start: `ocmar-state <dir> init <plan> <base> --owner <session-id>` (records plan-hash, base, initial content-fingerprint, owner)
- before each dispatch / on resume: `ocmar-state <dir> acquire --owner <session-id>` (flock + owner TTL check; BUSY → STOP and ask user)
- on task transition: `ocmar-state <dir> transition <task-id> <from> <to> [--review <p>]` (recomputes fingerprint in the same atomic write)
- batch-promote reviewed tasks: `ocmar-state <dir> promote-batch <task-id>...` (single transaction)
- heartbeat while a long task runs: `ocmar-state <dir> heartbeat`
- record a review/gate: `ocmar-state <dir> add-gate '{...}'`
- at skill start (resume): `ocmar-state <dir> resume-check` **before `acquire`** (verifies plan-hash + base + content-fingerprint; owner check = no conflicting live owner — a live owner that is not you, or a live owner when you have not identified yourself, → exit 13 STOP; a previous owner that released or went stale does NOT block). Returns `COMPLETED` (all verified) / `READY_VERIFY` (all reviewed — run the final whole-branch _priv-oracle-grok, then on APPROVED the verifier + promote-batch; do NOT skip the final _priv-oracle-grok, do NOT re-dispatch implementers) / `RESUME <task-id>` (first implementing/unstarted) / `INCOMPLETE` (no tasks). Any mismatch → exit 13 → STOP, do NOT auto-dispatch)
- on finishing: `ocmar-state <dir> release --finished --summary "<...>"`
- **before dispatching each implementer (pre-task snapshot):** `ocmar-state <dir> checkpoint <task-id>` — run immediately after `transition <task-id> <from> implementing`, while the tree still equals the ledger fingerprint. Refuses (exit 12) if WIP exists that the ledger doesn't reflect. This snapshot is what `restore` rebuilds from after a kill.
- **on resume of a killed implementer (resume-check → RESUME <task-id>):** `ocmar-state <dir> restore <task-id>` — rebuilds the tree to the pre-task snapshot, discarding the killed implementer's half-finished WIP; the ledger task stays `implementing`, revision unchanged. Then dispatch a fresh implementer.

**Owner identity:** the conductor sets `OCMAR_OWNER_SESSION=<this-session-id>` (or passes `--owner <session-id>`) for every mutating ocmar-state call (transition/promote-batch/add-task/set-task-field/add-gate/set-waiver/heartbeat/release). Non-owner calls exit 12.

content-fingerprint = sha256 over `git diff --binary --full-index <base>` + per-untracked-file `"<mode>\0<path>\0<sha256(content)>"` (NUL-separated, mode via lstat). Recomputed by `ocmar-state` on every transition — the fingerprint always matches the state it accompanies.

**At skill start (resume):** run `ocmar-state <dir> resume-check` **first, then `acquire`**. resume-check recomputes plan-hash, confirms base, recomputes content-fingerprint, and checks owner as **"no conflicting live owner"** — a previous owner that has `release`d or whose heartbeat is stale (TTL expired) does NOT block resume; only a *live* owner that differs from the caller, **or a live owner when the caller has not identified itself** (`OCMAR_OWNER_SESSION`/`--owner`), triggers exit 13 (→ STOP and ask the user, do NOT auto-dispatch). Its output drives the next step:
- `COMPLETED` — all tasks `verified`: workflow done, go to finishing.
- `READY_VERIFY` — no `implementing` task but `reviewed` tasks exist. **First confirm the final whole-branch `_priv-oracle-grok` returned `VERDICT=APPROVED` with zero Critical/Important** (check the gate record); only then dispatch the fresh verifier + `ocmar-state promote-batch` on pass. If the final _priv-oracle-grok has not run or not APPROVED, run it first — do NOT skip straight to the verifier (review-first / verify-last). Do NOT re-dispatch implementers (there is no task-id in this signal precisely to prevent that).
